10:32:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Color is ultimately about the **light that reaches your eyes**, not necessarily about reflection. With ordinary objects, light from the sun or a lamp hits the object. The material absorbs some wavelengths and reflects others. For example, a red apple reflects more red light than blue or green light, so it looks red. A screen works differently: it usually **creates or controls light directly**. ### Pixels and subpixels A screen is made of tiny dots called **pixels**. Each pixel is usually divided into three even smaller parts: - **Red** subpixel - **Green** subpixel - **Blue** subpixel By changing how bright each of those three subpixels is, the screen can make your visual system perceive many different colors. For example: - Red on, green and blue off → red - Green on, red and blue off → green - Red + green → yellow - Green + blue → cyan - Red + blue → magenta - Red + green + blue at high brightness → white - All off → black (or nearly black) This is called **additive color mixing**: adding colored light makes brighter, lighter colors. ### Why only red, green, and blue? Human eyes have three main types of color-sensitive cone cells, roughly most responsive to long, medium, and short wavelengths. Screens exploit this: by sending carefully chosen amounts of red, green, and blue light, they can produce the same cone responses as many other combinations of wavelengths. So the yellow from a screen usually is not the same physical light spectrum as sunlight reflected from a yellow object. But it stimulates your eyes in a similar enough way that your brain sees both as yellow. This is why a screen can show millions of colors using just three primary colored subpixels. ### LCD versus OLED screens Different screen technologies produce that light differently: - **LCD screens** have a bright white backlight behind the display. Each pixel contains tiny liquid-crystal shutters and red, green, and blue filters. The liquid crystals control how much backlight passes through each colored filter. - The backlight is still on even when the image is dark, which is why LCD blacks can look slightly gray. - **OLED screens** have red, green, and blue (or sometimes other arrangements of) tiny light-emitting elements. Each pixel produces its own light. - To display black, those pixels can simply turn off, giving very deep blacks. - **Older CRT TVs** used an electron beam to excite tiny red, green, and blue phosphor dots, causing them to glow. ### Why does it look smooth rather than like dots? The red, green, and blue subpixels are extremely small and close together. From normal viewing distance, your eye cannot resolve them separately, so it blends their light into one perceived color. If you look at a screen through a magnifying glass, you can often see the individual red, green, and blue components. In short: physical objects usually get their color by **reflecting selected light**, while screens get their color by **emitting or filtering controlled amounts of red, green, and blue light** directly into your eyes.
