10:33:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Reflection is one very common way that colored light gets into your eyes, but it is not the only way. A simple test: turn on a red LED, TV, or phone screen in a completely dark room. You can still see red. There is nothing for it to reflect light from—the device itself is sending light toward your eyes. The distinction is: - A **red-painted object** usually looks red because it reflects more red light from a lamp or the Sun. - A **red screen pixel** looks red because it emits red light, or, in an LCD, lets red light from its built-in backlight pass through. In both cases, what matters to your vision is the same: red-ish light enters your eye. Other non-reflection examples include: - the Sun and stars - flames - neon signs - LEDs - lightning - glowing hot metal - fluorescent materials under UV light “Color” is the visual sensation your brain produces based on the mix of light reaching your eye. Reflection is a mechanism that changes light before it reaches you; emission is a mechanism that produces light in the first place. Screens are basically millions of controllable tiny light sources. They use red, green, and blue light because mixtures of those can produce many color sensations for human eyes.
