10:33:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are right that an LCD uses materials to modify light from a backlight. A colored LCD pixel is similar in one respect to shining white light through colored glass: the material allows some wavelengths through and removes others. But that process is mainly **transmission and absorption**, not reflection. For example, in an LCD: 1. A white LED backlight produces light. 2. The LCD layer controls how much of that light gets through. 3. A red color filter transmits mostly red wavelengths and absorbs much of the green and blue. 4. The red light that passes through travels outward from the screen to your eye. Some light may indeed reflect or scatter inside the screen, as a side effect of real materials. But it is not the mechanism that makes a red pixel look red to you. The relevant light is traveling *through* the red filter toward you, not bouncing off it toward you. Compare three cases: - **Red paint:** White light hits it; red light is preferentially reflected back toward you. - **Red stained glass:** White light hits it from behind; mostly red light is transmitted through it toward you. - **LCD red subpixel:** White backlight is behind a red filter; mostly red light is transmitted through it toward you. All three involve material-dependent interactions with light. But only the first is primarily reflection. And the backlight itself is still an emitting source: its LEDs produce light electrically. If there were no backlight, an ordinary LCD would not display an image in a dark room. The filters cannot make light on their own; they only select which parts of the backlight’s light reach your eyes.
