10:34:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You can use “reflection” loosely in casual conversation to mean “light interacting with stuff,” and people may understand what you mean. But in physics, it is useful not to call all of it reflection, because the mechanisms behave differently: - **Reflection:** light returns from a surface. - **Transmission:** light passes through a material. - **Absorption:** light energy is taken up by the material. - **Scattering:** light is redirected in many directions. - **Emission:** the material produces new light using electrical, thermal, or chemical energy. For an LCD, the important path is mostly: **electrical energy → LED backlight emits light → filters/liquid crystals control it → colored light travels to your eye** Calling that “reflection” would obscure the key fact that an LCD can shine in a pitch-black room. The screen is not merely redirecting ambient light; it is powered and generates its own illumination. So your broad intuition is right—materials and light interactions are involved—but “reflection” has a narrower technical meaning than “anything materials do to light.”
