10:35:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It doesn’t mean you can’t trust yourself. It means you had a useful simplified model that worked well in the situations you encountered most often. That is how science learning usually works: - First model: “Things fall down.” - Better model: gravity pulls objects toward Earth. - More complete model: gravity involves masses, motion, spacetime, and so on. The first model was not useless; it was just incomplete. Likewise: - Useful everyday model: “Objects get their color from reflected light.” - More complete model: “We perceive color from the light reaching our eyes; reflection is one major way that happens.” Your original idea correctly explains why paint, clothing, food, leaves, and walls have colors. It only needs an added category for sources that generate light themselves, such as screens and lamps. A good way to trust scientific knowledge is not to expect every belief to be perfectly complete. Instead, ask: 1. What situations does this explanation predict correctly? 2. Where does it stop working? 3. What broader explanation includes both the old cases and the new ones? The fact that you noticed a possible inconsistency and pushed on it is exactly the kind of thinking science relies on.
